Practice 4 grammar (EBNF): three types (i32, i64, bool), comparisons
(==, !=) and a semantic pass on top of the Practice 3 language.

Notation: | alternatives, [ x ] optional, { x } zero or more,
"quoted" terminals are token kinds the lexer emits verbatim,
unquoted names are non-terminals with a rule below.

program    ::= { statement } exit_stmt
statement  ::= decl | assign
decl       ::= type [ "mut" ] ident "{" expr "}"
type       ::= "i32" | "i64" | "bool"
assign     ::= ident ":=" expr
exit_stmt  ::= "exit" factor
expr       ::= arith [ ( "==" | "!=" ) arith ]
arith      ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }
term       ::= factor { "*" factor }
factor     ::= number | "true" | "false" | ident

Terminals ident and number come from the lexer as "identifier" and
"constant" tokens; how they are spelled is the lexer's rule, not the
grammar's. "i32", "i64", "bool", "mut", "exit", "true", "false" are the
lexer's "typename"/"specifier"/"statement"/"boolean" keywords, block
start/end, "==" / "!=" and ":=" are two-byte operator tokens, "+" "-" "*"
single-byte operator tokens, used here with the text they carry.

One statement per line; blank lines carry no tokens and are skipped
before this grammar ever sees them. exit_stmt is the last line, and
there is exactly one; the parser enforces this since EBNF has no way to
say "last line". exit takes only a factor (a constant, a boolean
literal or a variable), never an operation.

Semantics the grammar cannot express live in checker.py, not here:
- a name declared once, before its first use, and only a mut variable
  assigned (moved out of code generation in Practice 3, into the
  semantic pass in Practice 4);
- the type of every expression: + - * take two integers and widen to
  the wider type; == != take two integers (any mix) or two bools and
  produce a bool; an i32 value may widen into an i64, nothing else
  converts; a decimal constant gets the narrowest type it fits.
That * binds tighter than + and - is not semantics: it falls out of the
shape of arith/term/factor and needs no rule of its own. That == / !=
bind weaker than arithmetic and allow only one comparison per
expression is the shape of expr/arith, likewise no rule of its own.
